<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyrgi Tablets</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrgi">Pyrgi</a> Tablets are three gold plates of about 500 BCE, two inscribed in Etruscan and one in Phoenician, found in 1964 at ancient Pyrgi, on the Tyrrhenian coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>. They record the dedication of a temple by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thefarie-velianas">Thefarie Velianas</a>, ruler of nearby Caere, to the goddess called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a> in the Phoenician text and Uni in the Etruscan. Though not a word-for-word translation, the bilingual text helps scholars interpret Etruscan and shows Phoenician or Punic influence in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. They are in the National Etruscan Museum, Villa Giulia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyrgi-tablets.jpg" alt="Pyrgi Tablets" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lamine_d'oro_in_lingua_etrusca_e_fenicia_con_dedica_di_un_luogo_sacro_a_pyrgi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The tablets are the oldest historical source documents from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, earlier than Roman hegemony, and rare examples of texts in these languages. The Phoenician inscription, known as KAI 277, is addressed to the Lady, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>. It says that the holy place was made by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thefarie-velianas">Thefarie Velianas</a>, king over the Caerites, during the month of the sacrifice to the Sun, and that he built it because Astarte asked it of him, in the third year of his reign, in the month of Krr, on the day of the burial of the divinity. It ends with a wish that the years of the goddess's statue in her temple be as many as the stars.</p><p>Phoenician is a Canaanite language and never needed decipherment, but some passages are uncertain and the last line has rival renderings. The Etruscan plates are harder still: scholars dispute nearly every word. They appear to speak of a temple and sacred buildings requested by Uni, of feasts in named months, and of years as many as the stars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Pallottino argued that the bilingual text suggests an attempt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> to support or impose a ruler over Caere when Etruscan sea power was waning. The Phoenician root MLK refers to sole power, but the Etruscan text does not use the word for king, lauχum. It uses zilac, magistrate, which suggests that Velianas may have been a tyrant of the kind found in some Greek cities. Building a temple and claiming to have been addressed by a god may have been ways of legitimating his power.</p><p>The tablets also support the accounts of Carthaginian involvement in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, who reports an ancient treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> dated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulships</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-junius-brutus">Lucius Junius Brutus</a> and Lucius Tarquinius Collatinus in 509 BCE, and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, in his account of the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalia">Alalia</a>. Until the tablets were found, no contemporary text from the area supported these isolated reports.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The text matters for the religion of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> around 500 BCE. It is generally assumed that the day of the burial of the divinity refers to the commemoration of the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adonis">Adonis</a>, and Schmitz has proposed reading another Phoenician phrase as at the death of the Handsome one, meaning Adonis. With the evidence for the rite of Adonia in the seventh column of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liber-linteus">Liber Linteus</a>, this makes it likely that the ritual was practised in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> from about 500 BCE into the second century BCE. Adonis himself is not named in either text.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pyrgi Tablets” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Among the key inscriptions are the sarcophagi of Ahiram, Tabnit and Eshmunazar II, the Bodashtart inscriptions and the Temple of Eshmun, the Kilamuwa Stela, the Karatepe bilingual, the Çineköy inscription, the Nora Stone, the Mdina Steles, the Cippi of Melqart and the <b>Pyrgi Tablets</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
